    <main>
        <h1>Operating Systems Overview</h1>

        <figure class="page-graphic">
            <img src="/img/os-overview.svg" alt="Diagram showing the operating system between applications and computer hardware">
            <figcaption>The operating system connects applications with the computer's hardware.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>The software behind your computer</h2>
        <p>An operating system turns computer hardware into a system people can use. It works between applications and the physical machine, coordinating resources and providing services that programs need.</p>

        <h2>What does an operating system do?</h2>
        <h3>Shares computer resources</h3>
        <p>The operating system coordinates the processor, memory, storage, and connected devices so programs can use them.</p>

        <h3>Lets people interact with computers</h3>
        <p>A graphical desktop, command line, or touchscreen gives people a way to tell a computer what to do.</p>

        <h3>Provides a foundation for applications</h3>
        <p>Applications rely on operating-system services instead of having to manage every hardware detail themselves.</p>

        <aside class="note">
            <h2>Why it matters</h2>
            <p>Without an operating system, every application would need to know how to operate each kind of hardware on its own.</p>
        </aside>
        <section class="book-source">
            <h2>Book source</h2>
            <p>For further reading: <a href="https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/">Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces</a> by Remzi H. Arpaci-Dusseau and Andrea C. Arpaci-Dusseau.</p>
        </section>
        <p><a href="/how-it-works">Next: learn how an operating system works</a></p>
    </main>

